##
    Where a name lives, and how long it lives there.

    Three things that shape a program without appearing in its output: a block that
    exists only to bound a name, a function declared inside another and able to see what
    surrounds it, and the word that says how far a type can be seen.

    Nothing here is exotic. It is the machinery every other sample sits on, gathered in one
    place so it can be watched.
##

##
    @summary: A list of amounts, reachable anywhere in its project and nowhere beyond.

    @remarks: 'internal' is what a type says when that is the reach it wants. A type saying
    nothing is internal already — writing it is for when a reader should not have to know the
    default.
##
internal model Ledger

    # @summary: Every amount added so far.
    integer[] amounts;

    # @summary: Opens an empty ledger.
    public function Ledger()
        this.amounts = {};
    end function

    # @summary: Records one amount.
    # @amount: what to add to the ledger.
    public function Add(integer amount)
        this.amounts.Insert(amount);
    end function

    # @summary: Adds up everything recorded.
    # @yields: the total of every amount.
    public integer function Total()
        integer running = 0;

        loop each amount in this.amounts
            running = running + amount;
        end loop

        yield running;
    end function
end model

shared model Program

    function Main()
        Program.Blocks();
        Program.NestedFunctions();
        Program.Capturing();

        Ledger ledger = new Ledger();

        ledger.Add(40);
        ledger.Add(2);

        Console.WriteLine("== a type only this project can see ==");
        Console.WriteLine("  total: " + ledger.Total());
    end function

    ##
        @summary: A block that exists only to bound a name.

        @remarks: 'begin' opens a scope and nothing else. It runs no loop and asks no question
        — it draws a line around a name so the name ends at the line. Useful when two stretches
        of a function each want a working value and neither should reach the other's.
    ##
    function Blocks()
        Console.WriteLine("== a block that only bounds a name ==");

        begin
            integer scratch = 6 * 7;
            Console.WriteLine("  inside:  " + scratch);
        end

        begin
            string scratch = "a different one entirely";
            Console.WriteLine("  inside:  " + scratch);
        end

        # Neither 'scratch' reaches here, and the two never met each other.
        Console.WriteLine("  outside: neither name survives the block");
    end function

    ##
        @summary: A function declared inside another.

        @remarks: Its name is reachable only within the function that holds it, which is what
        to reach for when a helper is meaningful to one caller and to nothing else. Both forms
        are here: one that yields a result, and one that yields nothing.
    ##
    function NestedFunctions()
        Console.WriteLine("== a function inside a function ==");

        # @summary: Twice a number, reachable only inside this function.
        # @value: the number to double.
        # @yields: twice it.
        integer function Doubled(integer value)
            yield value * 2;
        end function

        # @summary: Writes a labeled number, and yields nothing.
        # @label: what to call it.
        # @value: the number to write.
        function Announce(string label, integer value)
            Console.WriteLine("  " + label + ": " + value);
        end function

        Announce("doubled 4", Doubled(4));
        Announce("doubled 21", Doubled(21));
    end function

    ##
        @summary: What a nested function can see around it.

        @remarks: The locals it sits among, as they are rather than as they were when it was
        written. The counter below is read at each call, not copied at the declaration.
    ##
    function Capturing()
        Console.WriteLine("== what a nested function can see ==");

        integer counted = 0;

        # @summary: Raises the surrounding counter, which it sees rather than copies.
        function Count()
            counted = counted + 1;
        end function

        Count();
        Count();
        Count();

        Console.WriteLine("  counted: " + counted);
    end function

end model
